## 6. specification.md document structure

Generate the specification using the following structure.

# Software Requirements Specification

## Document Metadata

- Requirement ID:
- Requirement Title:
- Document Version:
- Status: Draft
- Created:
- Last Updated:
- Based On: discovery.md

## 1. Introduction

### 1.1 Purpose

Explain the purpose of the specification.

### 1.2 Scope

Define the functionality covered by the specification.

### 1.3 Intended Audience

Identify relevant readers, such as:

- Product owners.
- Business stakeholders.
- Software architects.
- Developers.
- Test engineers.
- Other relevant stakeholders.

### 1.4 References

List the discovery document and relevant project documentation.

## 2. Requirement Context

### 2.1 Problem Summary

Summarize the problem established during discovery.

### 2.2 Expected Outcome

Describe the intended outcome.

### 2.3 Actors and Stakeholders

Identify relevant actors and their responsibilities.

### 2.4 Definitions and Terminology

Define important domain-specific terms and abbreviations.

## 3. Scope and Boundaries

### 3.1 In Scope

### 3.2 Out of Scope

### 3.3 Existing Behavior to Preserve

### 3.4 Related Functionality

## 4. Functional Requirements

Organize requirements using unique FR identifiers.

For each requirement include:

- ID.
- Name.
- Description.
- Source.
- Actor.
- Preconditions.
- Trigger.
- Required behavior.
- Expected result.
- Related business rules.
- Relevant validation scenarios.
- Priority, if established.

## 5. Business Rules

Organize business rules using unique BR identifiers.

For each rule include:

- ID.
- Name.
- Description.
- Applicability.
- Expected behavior.
- Related functional requirements.
- Source.

## 6. Data Requirements

Describe relevant information and data contracts.

Include applicable:

- Inputs.
- Outputs.
- Required fields.
- Optional fields.
- Formats.
- Allowed values.
- Constraints.
- Validation rules.
- Sensitive information considerations.

## 7. System Behavior and Scenarios

Describe the relevant system interactions.

Include:

- Main success scenarios.
- Alternative scenarios.
- Validation failures.
- Authorization failures.
- Relevant exceptional conditions.
- Important edge cases.

Use structured scenarios or Given/When/Then when appropriate.

## 8. Validation and Error Handling Requirements

Document relevant validation conditions and expected observable error behavior.

Do not prescribe internal exception handling or implementation mechanisms.

## 9. Security and Authorization Requirements

Document relevant:

- Authentication requirements.
- Authorization requirements.
- Ownership rules.
- Access restrictions.
- Sensitive data handling.
- Security-related failure scenarios.

## 10. Non-Functional Requirements

Organize requirements using unique NFR identifiers.

Group them by relevant quality attribute.

For each requirement include:

- ID.
- Category.
- Description.
- Verification method.
- Target, if established.
- Source.

## 11. Acceptance Criteria

Organize acceptance criteria using unique AC identifiers.

Use Given/When/Then whenever appropriate.

Each criterion must reference the requirements it verifies.

## 12. Requirement Dependencies

Document relevant dependencies between requirements.

Do not include implementation task sequencing.

## 13. Assumptions and Open Questions

Clearly distinguish assumptions from confirmed decisions.

List unresolved questions and their impact.

## 14. Constraints and External Dependencies

Document relevant business, contractual, technical, regulatory, or integration constraints established by discovery or approved decisions.

## 15. Risks and Considerations

Identify relevant requirement-level risks.

## 16. Traceability Matrix

Connect discovery decisions, requirements, business rules, and acceptance criteria.

Example:

| Discovery reference | Requirement | Acceptance criteria |
|---|---|---|
| Decision D-001 | FR-001 | AC-001 |
| Decision D-002 | BR-001 | AC-002 |

Use only real references.

Include NFR and other relevant relationships when applicable.

## 17. Specification Quality Review

Summarize the validation performed.

Include:

- Functional completeness.
- Non-functional completeness.
- Acceptance coverage.
- Traceability.
- Consistency.
- Outstanding issues.

## 18. Specification Readiness

Describe whether the specification is sufficiently complete for technical planning.

List unresolved issues that may affect the next stage.

## 19. Approval

- Specification proposal approved: Yes/No
- Final specification approved: Yes/No
- Approved by: User
- Approval date:

