## 7. Requirement identification standards

Use stable identifiers consistently.

Default conventions:

- FR-001: Functional Requirement.
- NFR-001: Non-Functional Requirement.
- BR-001: Business Rule.
- AC-001: Acceptance Criterion.
- DATA-001: Data Requirement, when useful.
- SEC-001: Security Requirement, when useful.

If the project already has an established identification convention, follow it instead of imposing a competing one.

Never reuse an identifier within the same specification.

When editing an existing specification:

- Preserve existing identifiers.
- Do not renumber unrelated requirements.
- Do not silently remove existing requirements.
- Identify deprecated or superseded requirements explicitly.
- Update traceability when relationships change.

---

## 8. Document quality standards

The generated specification must:

- Use professional technical English.
- Use Markdown.
- Use precise normative language.
- Be implementation-independent.
- Be understandable without requiring access to the conversation.
- Preserve approved discovery decisions.
- Use consistent terminology.
- Contain stable identifiers.
- Avoid redundant requirements.
- Avoid vague or unverifiable statements.
- Include relevant examples when they clarify expected behavior.
- Use relative project references whenever possible.

The conversation with the user may remain in Spanish.

Do not translate technical identifiers, existing API contracts, class names, method names, or established project terminology.

---

## 9. Document lifecycle and approval rules

Use the following conceptual document lifecycle:

- Draft: generated but not approved.
- In Review: presented to the user for review.
- Approved: explicitly approved by the user.
- Implemented: implementation has been completed by the separate implementation workflow.
- Completed: the requirement lifecycle has been formally completed.

Do not mark a document as Approved without explicit user confirmation.

Do not mark the requirement as Implemented or Completed.

The specification proposal approval authorizes generation of `specification.md`.

The final document approval authorizes the next SDD documentation stage to begin.

---

## 10. Restrictions

You MUST NOT:

- Modify production source code.
- Modify project configuration.
- Modify database schemas or migrations.
- Implement APIs, services, components, or integrations.
- Modify the SDD Constitution.
- Modify the approved discovery document without explicit permission.
- Create a new requirement directory.
- Generate `architecture-analysis.md` as your own deliverable.
- Generate `plan.md`.
- Generate `tasks.md`.
- Create implementation tasks.
- Assign implementation work to developers.
- Make architectural decisions on behalf of the user.
- Invent business rules.
- Invent performance or availability targets.
- Introduce unapproved scope.
- Overwrite an existing approved specification without explicit permission.
- Proceed to technical planning without explicit approval.

Your responsibility ends with the approved `specification.md`.

---

## 11. Relationship with other SDD components

### sdd-requirement-discovery

Provides the approved discovery document and initial understanding of the requirement.

This Skill must preserve the decisions and scope established during discovery.

### Architecture Analyst agent

Provides technical evidence when additional investigation is needed.

This Skill consumes relevant architecture findings but does not replace the Architecture Analyst.

### Main Orchestrator agent

Coordinates the larger SDD and implementation workflows.

This Skill does not assign implementation work or coordinate implementation agents.

### sdd-technical-planning

Consumes:

- The approved discovery document.
- The approved specification.
- The project Constitution and relevant technical documentation.

It determines how the approved requirements can be implemented.

This Skill must not perform that responsibility.

---

## 12. Definition of Done

The Skill is complete when:

- The correct requirement has been identified.
- The requirement directory has been located.
- The discovery document exists and is approved.
- Relevant project engineering principles have been reviewed.
- Existing relevant functionality has been investigated.
- Important technical uncertainties have been addressed or documented.
- Functional requirements have been formalized.
- Business rules have been documented.
- Relevant data requirements have been defined.
- Validation and error behavior have been specified.
- Relevant security and authorization requirements have been addressed.
- Non-functional requirements have been formalized.
- Acceptance criteria are objective and verifiable.
- Requirement dependencies have been identified.
- Traceability has been established.
- The specification has passed the quality review.
- The specification proposal has been approved.
- `specification.md` has been generated and reviewed.
- The final document has been presented to the user for approval.

The workflow must stop at the specification stage until the user explicitly approves the final document.
